A||N||11||BlackDragon||how important is the egr? can it be blocked off?||||1||
Z||000000||BlackDragon||08-12-2001||06:32 PM||||and will it hurt anything? 84 l-4. what are the effects, if any?||||reg||1
Z||000001||johnt671||08-12-2001||07:11 PM||||Ive had mine plugged off and on over 2 yrs. without any problems.  Gives a bit better throttle response and keeps crap out of your engine.  Mine's an 84 also.||||reg||1
Z||000002||saleenfiero||08-12-2001||07:27 PM||||How is this plugged?||||reg||1
Z||000003||88formula||08-12-2001||07:42 PM||||The side effects are detonation, which can lead to piston failure and higher NOX emissions. If you disconnect it you may end up needing to use a high octane fuel to help prevent detonation.   <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||88formula||08-12-2001||07:43 PM||||It doesn’t effect full throttle acceleration because the computer disables it anyway under this condition. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||johnt671||08-12-2001||07:48 PM||||I just plug the vacuum line to disable the EGR. I've always run on plus gas so I didn't notice a ping problem.<p>[This message has been edited by johnt671 (edited 08-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||BlackDragon||08-12-2001||08:07 PM||||thanx for all the input||||reg||1
Z||000007||862M4inCA||08-13-2001||10:36 AM||||hrm...plugged egr. Yes there are benefits to disabling the egr system such as better response and in some cases better milage. However I had a nasty experience with a disabled egr that resulted in a new motor.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">Maybe I did it wrong since I just removed the vacuum line (I have a 4 cyl so the EMC doesn't really control it's operation) and plugged it off. Was taking a short trip to LA with a friend and while climbing out of the central valley of CA which anyone who has been there realizes it can be a steep climb. Well to make a long story short not having the "cooling effect" that the egr can provide running into the cylinders I got about half way up the climb when my engine started running VERY rough. Pulled to the side of the road popped the deck lid (engine still running at this point) noticed a VERY red catalytic converter and black exhaust. After getting towed 60 miles back home and tearing into the motor I learned that I had melted down my #3 piston and the others weren't looking exactly healthy either. Now running better gas might help, however I used premium (as always since I find I get MUCH better performance with it) and had these results. Needless to say for me, plugging the EGR isn't something I wanna do again.   just my $.02||||reg||1
Z||000008||qwikgta||08-14-2001||11:13 PM||||I have never understood how adding exhaust gas into the system can be a good thing, you would think that blocking it off would only hurt your emmisions and improve performance.  ||||reg||1
Z||000009||BlackDragon||08-14-2001||11:21 PM||||that is what i thought, but aperantly it helps cool the motor||||reg||1
Z||000010||terryk||08-15-2001||12:18 AM||||Well, it does. It reduced the combustion temperatures and reduced Nox. To make the car run right, they add fuel and advance.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Capt Fiero||08-15-2001||12:25 AM||||Quick exp on EGR and why it does what it does.  When you have a mix of fuel and air, you get a burn temp, when you add exhaust gas it displaces some of the air in the mix and now you have less air and the same amount of fuel, when you have less air it wont burn as hot or fast.  Same thing as an Oxy Acetelen Torch, if you light the torch and get a nice burn then start cranking up the O2 it will get a great deal hotter.  I hope that explains it in basic info for you.  The chips that shut off the EGR also increase your fuel map so that you dont have that lean out condition. <P>------------------<BR>David<BR>85GT<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://photos.onebigvillage.com/pubimage.asp?id_=32415">  <p>[This message has been edited by Capt Fiero (edited 08-15-2001).]||||reg||1
